q('What is the central cellular defect in classical PNH?', 'An acquired stem-cell clone with impaired GPI-anchor synthesis, commonly from a somatic PIGA mutation',[
 ('An inherited beta-globin substitution in all cells','That is a hemoglobinopathy mechanism.'),('A single anti-Rh antibody coating normal red cells','PNH is not primarily an alloimmune disorder.'),('An acquired absence of all plasma complement proteins','The cells are vulnerable because protective surface proteins are deficient.')],
 'Loss of membrane protection makes cells complement-sensitive', '**GPI-anchor deficiency reduces surface proteins such as CD55 and CD59.**',['The defect can affect more than the red-cell lineage.','Clinical disease includes varying combinations of hemolysis, thrombosis and marrow failure.'], '**Distinguish the clone from its clinical consequences.**',['A small asymptomatic population does not automatically need the same treatment as classical PNH.','Assess hemolysis and thrombotic history.'],[['Clone','Acquired hematopoietic stem-cell origin'],['Surface defect','GPI-linked protein deficiency'],['Consequences','Complement vulnerability and disease-specific risks']],[PN,PC])
q('Which diagnostic approach is preferred for detecting and characterizing a PNH clone?', 'High-sensitivity multiparameter flow cytometry using appropriate lineage markers and GPI-associated probes',[
 ('Ham test as the current preferred definitive assay','Flow cytometry has superseded older lysis tests.'),('A positive urine dipstick as complete confirmation','Hemoglobinuria is not specific and may be absent.'),('RBC morphology alone','The clone is defined by surface-protein deficiency, not a unique routine shape.')],
 'Use lineage-specific flow assessment rather than a single nonspecific marker', '**FLAER and GPI-linked markers are useful in white-cell lineages.**',['Red-cell analysis uses appropriate markers such as CD59.','Hemolysis or recent transfusion can underestimate the RBC clone.'], '**Interpret multiple populations and assay sensitivity.**',['Granulocyte and monocyte assessment helps estimate the underlying clone.','Technical expertise is important for very small populations.'],[['WBC testing','FLAER plus appropriate markers'],['RBC testing','Appropriate GPI-linked surface markers'],['Confounders','Hemolysis and transfusion']],[PN])
q('An adult with DAT-negative intravascular hemolysis develops hepatic-vein thrombosis and abdominal pain. Which disease should be specifically investigated?', 'PNH',[
 ('Uncomplicated iron deficiency as the complete explanation','It does not integrate intravascular hemolysis and unusual-site thrombosis.'),('Hereditary spherocytosis as the most characteristic vascular syndrome','This is not its classic combined presentation.'),('Pure red-cell aplasia','It causes low production rather than this hemolytic/thrombotic pattern.')],
 'Unusual-site thrombosis can be a clue to a complement-sensitive clone', '**PNH can cause splanchnic or cerebral venous thrombosis.**',['The diagnosis does not require nocturnal-only hemoglobinuria.','Marrow cytopenias can coexist.'], '**Confirm by appropriate flow testing and manage the emergency.**',['Anticoagulation and complement-directed treatment require specialist assessment.','Assess bleeding risk and the full marrow-failure context.'],[['Hemolysis','Often DAT negative'],['Thrombosis','May occur at unusual venous sites'],['Confirmation','PNH flow cytometry']],[PC,PN],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A PNH patient receiving effective C5 inhibition has controlled intravascular hemolysis but persistent anemia with evidence of C3-associated extravascular red-cell clearance. Which interpretation is most appropriate?', 'Proximal complement activity can contribute to residual extravascular hemolysis despite terminal-pathway blockade',[
 ('C5 inhibition guarantees correction of every cause of anemia','Marrow failure, deficiency and extravascular clearance can persist.'),('The anemia proves the PNH clone has disappeared','It does not establish clone eradication.'),('C3 coating is impossible during terminal complement blockade','Upstream complement activity can remain.')],
 'Blocking one complement level does not block every upstream effect', '**C5 inhibition prevents terminal-mediated lysis but can leave C3 opsonization.**',['Residual anemia still requires a complete differential.','Marrow failure and iron or nutrient problems may coexist.'], '**Use mechanism-based specialist reassessment.**',['Selected proximal inhibitors can address relevant residual hemolysis.','Do not change therapy from Hb alone without clarifying the cause.'],[['Terminal blockade','Controls intravascular lysis'],['Upstream activity','Can promote C3-associated clearance'],['Residual anemia','May have several causes']],[PC,IP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which complement component is directly targeted by iptacopan?', 'Factor B',[
 ('C1s','This is the sutimlimab target.'),('C5','This is the eculizumab/ravulizumab target.'),('CD20','This is a B-cell surface target, not a complement component.')],
 'Iptacopan targets the alternative-pathway amplification mechanism', '**Factor B inhibition affects proximal complement activity.**',['It is an oral treatment option for adults with PNH under the US label.','Do not assume its other disease indications use identical endpoints or eligibility.'], '**Follow the current vaccination and transition plan.**',['Treatment interruption can permit hemolysis recurrence.','Encapsulated-bacterial infection risk is a major safety consideration.'],[['Iptacopan','Factor B'],['Sutimlimab','C1s'],['Eculizumab / ravulizumab','C5']],[IP])
q('A vaccinated patient receiving a complement inhibitor develops fever and rapidly worsening systemic symptoms. What is the appropriate response?', 'Urgent assessment for serious infection, including encapsulated bacteria, despite vaccination',[
 ('Exclude meningococcal disease because vaccination eliminates the risk','Residual invasive infection risk remains.'),('Wait until the next scheduled visit','Rapid deterioration can be life-threatening.'),('Stop evaluation if the first white-cell count is normal','A normal count does not safely exclude serious infection.')],
 'Vaccination reduces risk but does not eliminate complement-inhibitor vulnerability', '**Serious meningococcal and other encapsulated-bacterial infections remain possible.**',['Follow the product-specific vaccine and prophylaxis guidance.','Teach patients to recognize warning symptoms.'], '**Treat suspected invasive infection promptly.**',['Use the local emergency and antimicrobial pathway.','Coordinate ongoing complement treatment decisions with the specialist team.'],[['Prevention','Vaccines and indicated prophylaxis'],['Residual risk','Still present'],['Fever / deterioration','Urgent evaluation']],[IP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An adult has severe normocytic anemia with almost absent reticulocytes, preserved neutrophils and platelets, and marked selective loss of marrow erythroid precursors. Which syndrome fits?', 'Pure red-cell aplasia',[
 ('Aplastic anemia affecting all lineages','The other lineages are preserved here.'),('Regenerative peripheral hemolysis','A profound reticulocyte deficit argues against adequate regenerative output.'),('Hypersplenism as the characteristic selective marrow finding','This does not explain selective erythroid aplasia.')],
 'Selective lineage failure differs from global marrow aplasia', '**PRCA is characterized by severe erythroid underproduction.**',['Acquired causes include immune disease, thymoma, infection and drugs.','Large granular lymphocytic disorders can be associated.'], '**Search for a cause before selecting immunosuppression.**',['Review viral risk, medicines and chest imaging when appropriate.','Transfusion support may be necessary while treatment takes effect.'],[['Blood','Anemia with profound reticulocytopenia'],['Other lineages','Often preserved'],['Marrow','Selective erythroid precursor loss']],[PR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An immunocompromised patient has persistent severe reticulocytopenic anemia, marrow erythroid aplasia and parvovirus B19 DNA detected. Which treatment is an important option?', 'Intravenous immunoglobulin with assessment of the underlying immunosuppression',[
 ('Iron chelation as the antiviral treatment','It does not treat persistent parvovirus infection.'),('Rituximab automatically as the initial antiviral strategy','Further B-cell suppression can be inappropriate in this setting.'),('No treatment because parvovirus is always self-limited in every host','Persistent infection occurs in immunocompromised patients.')],
 'Persistent viral suppression differs from a brief aplastic crisis', '**Parvovirus can chronically suppress erythropoiesis when humoral immunity is impaired.**',['PCR is useful in this setting.','Serology may be less reliable in a severely immunocompromised host.'], '**Use cause-directed therapy and monitor for relapse.**',['IVIG can supply neutralizing antibodies.','Adjust immunosuppression where feasible with the treating service.'],[['Host','Impaired immune clearance'],['Evidence','PRCA plus viral DNA'],['Treatment','IVIG and immune-context review']],[PR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient is diagnosed with acquired PRCA. Which associated structural lesion should be considered?', 'Thymoma',[
 ('An isolated gallstone as the defining association','That is more a chronic hemolysis complication.'),('A congenital pulmonary bleb','This is not the characteristic PRCA association.'),('A renal calculus as proof of the cause','That does not establish a PRCA mechanism.')],
 'A cause-focused PRCA evaluation includes the mediastinum', '**Thymoma is a recognized association.**',['Myasthenia or other immune manifestations may coexist.','The evaluation also includes drugs, infection and lymphoid disorders.'], '**Treat the tumor and the hematological process as indicated.**',['Resection does not guarantee complete PRCA resolution.','Immune-directed treatment can still be necessary.'],[['Association','Thymoma'],['Possible overlap','Other autoimmune disease'],['Management','Lesion treatment plus PRCA assessment']],[PR])
q('An adult has persistent fever, splenomegaly, progressive cytopenias, hypertriglyceridemia, falling fibrinogen and marked hyperferritinemia. Which syndrome requires urgent assessment?', 'Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis',[
 ('Iron overload proven by ferritin alone','The inflammatory multisystem constellation requires a different assessment.'),('Uncomplicated nutritional iron deficiency','This does not explain the full syndrome.'),('A normal immune response requiring no evaluation','Progressive organ dysfunction can be life-threatening.')],
 'HLH is a hyperinflammatory syndrome with multiple possible triggers', '**The constellation is more informative than any isolated marker.**',['Infections, malignancy and rheumatic disease are important adult causes.','Sepsis and HLH can overlap.'], '**Evaluate the trigger and inflammatory severity together.**',['Use HScore and/or an appropriate criteria framework with judgment.','Early specialist treatment may be needed before every delayed test returns.'],[['Pattern','Fever, cytopenias and organ involvement'],['Laboratory clues','Ferritin, triglycerides and fibrinogen'],['Work-up','Trigger and syndrome assessment']],[HL,HI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A marrow aspirate in a patient strongly suspected of HLH does not show hemophagocytosis. Which conclusion is correct?', 'HLH is not excluded; hemophagocytosis can be absent early and is not independently specific when present',[
 ('Absence of hemophagocytosis definitively excludes HLH','Sensitivity is insufficient for that rule.'),('Presence of any hemophagocytosis alone confirms HLH','It occurs in other severe illnesses too.'),('No further clinical or laboratory assessment can contribute','The diagnosis integrates multiple features.')],
 'The syndrome is not defined by one marrow image', '**Hemophagocytosis is a supportive finding with limitations.**',['Timing and sampling affect detection.','Other inflammatory or infectious states can show it.'], '**Continue a probability-based urgent assessment.**',['Review the whole clinical trajectory and trigger evaluation.','Do not postpone necessary treatment solely for a repeat positive marrow finding.'],[['Absent','Does not exclude'],['Present','Not independently diagnostic'],['Decision','Integrated criteria and clinical judgment']],[HL,HI])
q('An adult has ferritin 18,000 micrograms/L during acute liver injury and sepsis. What is the best interpretation?', 'HLH should be considered, but this value alone is not diagnostic in adults',[
 ('Ferritin above 10,000 is pathognomonic for adult HLH','Specificity is lower in adults and competing severe illnesses can cause extreme values.'),('HLH is excluded because sepsis is present','The conditions can coexist.'),('The result proves hereditary hemochromatosis requiring immediate phlebotomy','Ferritin is not a direct proof of that diagnosis in acute inflammation and liver injury.')],
 'Extreme hyperferritinemia is an urgent clue, not a standalone label', '**Assess the broader inflammatory phenotype.**',['Cytopenias, organomegaly, fibrinogen, triglycerides and other findings contribute.','Serial clinical and laboratory change can clarify the probability.'], '**Treat the demonstrated emergencies while investigating the trigger.**',['Control infection and support organs.','When HLH is sufficiently likely and severe, use prompt specialist-directed immunomodulatory or trigger-specific treatment.'],[['Ferritin','Strong concern but nonspecific'],['Competing causes','Sepsis and liver injury'],['Action','Integrated urgent assessment and treatment']],[HL,HI],style='clinical_scenario')

